          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What follow-up interval does guidance describe after starting a new blood pressure medicine?",
          "answer": "The guidance I found describes a **monthly review** after starting a blood pressure medicine, continuing until the target is reached. Once blood pressure is stable, reviews move to **every three to six months**. (Source: *Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line*, major cardiology society consensus guidance, educational summary.)\n\nIf the new medicine acts on the renin-angiotensin system, that same document says kidney function and potassium should also be checked after starting. Review should also cover whether the person is taking the medicine as prescribed.\n\nI did not find a conflicting follow-up interval from another authority. The retrieved passages do describe disagreement between bodies, but on how aggressively to treat low-risk stage 1 hypertension, not on the review schedule. A separate heart failure guideline mentions reviews about every two weeks, but that applies to heart failure treatment, not routine blood pressure medicine, so I have not used it here.\n\nThe strength of the evidence behind these intervals was not included in the passages I retrieved, so I can't say how firmly the monthly schedule is supported. Your clinician can confirm the right schedule for your situation, including how soon after starting your medicine you should be seen.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
